Why I used "long long" instead of just "int":

A credit card number can have up to 16 digits. 
A normal number type in C++ (called int) can only safely hold about 9 or 10 digits before it starts giving wrong answers —
 kind of like trying to fit a big number into a box that's too small, and some of it "spills over" and gets lost. long long is just a bigger box, so the whole card number fits properly without getting messed up.

Why I broke the code into small pieces (functions):

Instead of writing one giant block of code that tries to do everything at once, I split the work into small helper tasks, where each one only does ONE simple job. This is like following a recipe step-by-step instead of trying to cook the whole meal in one confusing move. It's easier to check each step is correct, and easier to fix if something goes wrong.
